# Non-vacuity witness for the k-induction proof (dcache_kind.sby).
#
# Same defines, same geometry, same data abstraction. An unbounded proof
# under a data abstraction and a restricted geometry is only worth
# something if the environment can still reach the behaviour being proven
# about -- a read of the tracked word, and the eviction sequence the whole
# property turns on. BUG-007 was exactly this check never running.
#
# The proof's own rationale:
#
# WHY THIS, AND WHY NOW
# The bounded proof cannot work: the eviction sequence is first reachable at
# step 28 and cost grows ~4x per step, so the depth that would make a result
# meaningful is unreachable (see dcache.sby). PDR did not converge either
# (dcache_prove.sby). k-induction is the remaining option, and unlike PDR it
# lets the invariants be written by hand rather than discovered.
#
# It is S2 work, not P3 cleanup. COHERENCE.md 6: COH-INV-03 -- a read returns
# the most recent write by *either* core -- is a strictly harder version of
# this same property over two caches, so whatever closes this is the method
# the coherence proofs need. Getting it working here first means a failing
# induction step points at weak invariants rather than at hardware nobody has
# debugged yet.
#
# HOW INDUCTION DIFFERS FROM WHAT CAME BEFORE
# A bounded proof starts at reset and replays forward, which is why the
# eviction sequence costs what it does. Induction starts from an *arbitrary*
# state and shows the property is preserved for one more step. Reset never
# enters it, so depth stops being the problem. The new problem is that most
# arbitrary states are unreachable nonsense, and the invariants are what rule
# those out.
[options]
mode cover
depth 32
expect pass

[engines]
smtbmc boolector

[script]
read -sv -DDCACHE -DFV_DATA_ABSTRACT -DFORMAL cache_fv.sv
read -sv -DFORMAL cache.v
read -sv -DFORMAL sram_1p_bm.v
prep -top cache_fv -flatten
memory_map
opt -fast

[files]
cache_fv.sv
../../../rtl/cache/cache.v
../../models/sram_1p_bm.v
